/**
 * Physics Studio: joints, snap zones and live rope / spring lines, shared by the editor and every
 * runtime (scripts/sync-physics-studio.mjs copies this file into the other packages).
 *
 * Joints live on the artboard and connect two bodies (objects with physics on):
 *   artboard.physics.joints = [{ id, type, bodyA, bodyB, anchorA, anchorB, ... }]
 *   - type 'Rope'   : bodyB stays within `length` of bodyA (slack allowed; a lanyard, a hanging sign)
 *   - type 'Spring' : pulled back to `length`, `frequency` Hz with `dampingRatio` (0 = bounces forever, 1 = no bounce)
 *   - type 'Hinge'  : the anchors are pinned together and the bodies turn around them (`limits` in degrees)
 *   - type 'Slider' : bodyB slides along `axis` (in bodyA space) between `limits` (px)
 *   - type 'Weld'   : glued together as they are now
 *   Older files use 'Distance' (stiffness/damping), 'Revolute' and 'Prismatic'; they still work.
 *   Anchors are offsets from each body's centre in its own unrotated space (px). Joined bodies don't
 *   collide with each other unless `collide: true`. A pin is just a small Static body (often a sensor).
 *
 * Snap zones (on any object; the zone follows it):
 *   obj.physicsSnap = { radius: 80, strength: 1, rotate: true, match: 'tag', input: 'slotFilled' }
 *   - a released body that comes within `radius` is pulled in and held at the centre; grab it to take it out
 *   - match: only bodies whose physics.tags include this tag (or any of a list)
 *   - input: a state machine input set to true while the zone holds a body (false when empty)
 *
 * Link lines (on a Path): obj.physicsLink = { joint: 'jointId', look: 'rope' | 'spring' | 'line', coils?: 9, width?: 14 }
 *   The path is redrawn every frame between the joint's two anchors: a rope sags by its slack,
 *   a spring draws a coil that stretches and squashes. Links never take the pointer.
 */
